; host/loader_server.bl — the pinned HOST MODULE for `Elixir.BeamLisp.Loader.Server`.
;
; `lib/beam_lisp/loader_server.ex` is the pinned process that runs `:global`
; library loads. Libraries load once, VM-wide, at `:global` (see
; `BeamLisp.Loader`); a load's top-level forms may create process-owned state
; such as ETS tables, so routing source evaluation and AOT `__bl_init__/0`
; replay through this GenServer binds that state to the VM rather than to
; whichever process required it first. Nested requires run INLINE (a self-call
; would deadlock). Exceptions cross back to the caller with their ORIGINAL
; stacktrace and a `:throw` stays a `:throw`; the server survives both.
;
; This file is the `.bl` port of that module, compiled by
; `BeamLisp.AOT/compile_host_file` into ONE exact-named beam whose export
; surface is the live module's: `use GenServer` contributes `child_spec/1` and
; default implementations for the OPTIONAL callbacks (`handle_cast/2`,
; `handle_info/2`, `terminate/2`, `code_change/3`) — none of which the live
; module overrides, though it does override the generated `handle_call/3` — and
; the file adds `start_link/1`, `init/1`, `in_server?/0`, `run/1` and its own
; `handle_call/3`.
;
;   (This wave owns source and tests only; the seed entry that points a real
;   atom at this file is written by the wave that deletes `loader_server.ex`.
;   That entry needs `:behaviour :"Elixir.GenServer"` — the live beam carries
;   `-behaviour(GenServer)` because `use GenServer` does — and nothing else:
;   the module is functions-only, with no struct to synthesize and no macro to
;   export.)
;
; WHAT THE MODULE OWES, and what each line preserves:
;
;   * THE PROCESS DICTIONARY FLAG is the one piece of state that makes `run/1`
;     re-entrant. It is the literal key `:bl_in_loader_server` (the live
;     `@flag`), written with the Elixir `Process` module — NOT `:erlang` — for
;     two reasons: `Process.get/1` answers `nil` for an absent key where
;     `:erlang.get/1` answers the atom `:undefined` (and `:undefined` is TRUTHY
;     in this language, so the inline branch would always win), and
;     `Process.put/2`/`Process.delete/1` are the very calls the live module
;     makes. `run/1` tests it for TRUTH, exactly as `if Process.get(@flag)`
;     does: only `nil` and `false` are falsy here and in Elixir alike.
;
;   * THE FLAG'S LIFETIME is `put` before the thunk runs and `delete` after it,
;     whatever happened — the live `try … after Process.delete(@flag)`. The
;     `finally` runs on every exit path, including the exit re-raise below, so
;     a crash inside the thunk cannot leave the flag behind and strand the
;     server's next call on the inline branch.
;
;   * THE KIND IS READ, NOT RECONSTRUCTED. A pinned `.bl` `try/catch` binds one
;     NORMALIZED value `e`: the compiler emits a single Erlang
;     `catch Kind:Value:Stack` and folds the kind in — a `:throw` keeps its raw
;     value, an `:error` becomes `Exception.normalize(:error, …)` (so it is
;     ALWAYS an exception struct, non-exception reasons wrapped in
;     `%ErlangError{}`), and an `:exit` becomes the 2-tuple `{:exit, value}`.
;     The fold alone cannot tell a raw `:erlang.throw/1` of an exception struct
;     from a `raise` of that same struct — both normalize to that struct. So the
;     compiler also injects `__EXCEPTION_KIND__`, the RAW catch class
;     (`:throw`/`:error`/`:exit`), into every catch handler's lexical scope
;     (see `priv/boot/compiler.bl::catch-handler-node`). The classification in
;     `handle_call/3` switches on it: `:throw` answers `{:thrown, v}` with the
;     raw value — so a thrown exception struct stays a THROW, and one of an
;     `{:exit, v}` pair stays a throw, the two corners the old shape-guess
;     folded wrongly; `:error` answers `{:raised, e, __STACKTRACE__}`; `:exit`
;     is RE-RAISED as a true exit (the live `rescue`/`catch :throw` pair does
;     not catch exits, so the server dies there and must die here) through the
;     dynamic `:erlang.raise/3`, recovering the raw reason from the folded
;     `{:exit, value}`. The language's own `(throw x)` lowers to `erlang/error`
;     of a `BeamLisp.ExInfo` — an `:error` — so it never reaches the throw
;     corners; they exist for raw `:erlang.throw/1` callers, exactly as the
;     live module behaves.
;
;   * THE STACKTRACE IS THE FRAME LIST. Inside a catch handler the compiler
;     binds `__STACKTRACE__` — the same `{Module, Function, Arity, Location}`
;     list Elixir's own `__STACKTRACE__` holds — and it is carried out in the
;     `:raised` reply untouched. `run/1` re-raises with it through
;     `:erlang.raise/3`, so the caller sees the raise site, not this module.
;     The LIVE `reraise/2` is the macro `:erlang.raise(:error,
;     Kernel.Utils.raise(e), stacktrace)` — a VARIABLE argument takes the
;     macro's general branch, not its literal-message one — so the port calls
;     `Elixir.Kernel.Utils/raise/1` and then `:erlang.raise/3`, byte for byte
;     the same two steps.
;
;   * A `:throw` STAYS A `:throw`. The live `run/1` answers `throw(v)`, i.e.
;     `:erlang.throw/1`; the port calls the same BIF dynamically, never the
;     language's `(throw …)` (which lowers to `erlang/error` of an `ExInfo` and
;     would hand the caller an `:error` where the live module hands it a
;     `:throw`).
;
;   * RAISE REPLAYS PUBLIC FRAME LISTS. Dynamic `erlang:apply/3` invokes the
;     public `erlang:raise/3` BIF with `__STACKTRACE__`. Compiler-generated raw
;     catch rethrows carry a separate semantic IR marker; they alone use the
;     internal `raw_raise` primop.
;
;   * THE BOOT EDGE. This module is an OTP APPLICATION CHILD: `BeamLisp.Env` +
;     `BeamLisp.Loader.Server`, started by `BeamLisp.Application.start/2`
;     BEFORE `BeamLisp.init/0` interns one var. So no function here may resolve
;     a var in `BeamLisp.Env` or call `BeamLisp.Ns.Rt`: those reach the var
;     table `init/0` is what fills. Every call below is a HOST call —
;     `GenServer`, `Supervisor`, `Process`, `Kernel`, `String.Chars`, the
;     `Elixir.FunctionClauseError`/`RuntimeError` exceptions, and raw `erlang`
;     BIFs. `list`/`tuple` lower to direct calls on the `BeamLisp.Ns.Core`
;     beam, which is on the code path before the application starts (the same
;     shape `priv/host/application.bl` ships), never a var lookup.
;
;   * NO TOP-LEVEL HELPERS. A pinned module's export surface IS the file's
;     top-level defns (`defn-` is not privatized on this path), and this
;     module's surface must be the live module's and nothing more. Every helper
;     is therefore a LOCAL closure, and `self_module` — the atom this source
;     was compiled into, read from a fun literal compiled into this file — is
;     inlined at each use so the same bytes serve production and probe without
;     naming either.
;
;   * THE GENERATED DEFAULTS ARE PORTED, not dropped. `use GenServer` generates
;     a default implementation for every OPTIONAL callback, so the live module
;     exports all four even though it defines none of them, and a port missing
;     them would have a smaller ABI than the module it stands in for. They are
;     reproduced as the Elixir 1.20 macro writes them: `handle_cast/2`'s
;     `:erlang.phash2(1, 1)` trick (whose `0` branch — the only reachable one —
;     raises, and whose `1` branch is the `{:stop, {:bad_cast, msg}, state}`
;     Dialyzer sees), `handle_info/2`'s `:logger.error` report (same label,
;     same report map, same metadata, `GenServer.format_report/1` as the
;     `report_cb`), `terminate/2`'s `:ok`, and `code_change/3`'s
;     `{:ok, state}`.
;
;   * THE CLAUSE HEADS. The live `init(:ok)`, `run/1` (`when is_function(fun,
;     0)`) and `handle_call({:run, fun}, …)` each have exactly one clause, so a
;     non-matching argument is a `FunctionClauseError` raised BY THE BEAM. The
;     port keeps the same heads as explicit `if` bodies and raises the same
;     `FunctionClauseError` (module/function/arity) by hand, because a pinned
;     `.bl` parameter pattern compiles to a body-level match (a `MatchError`),
;     not to a clause head — the `priv/host/lazy_seq.bl` idiom.
;
;   * `child_spec/1` IS LOAD-BEARING. `BeamLisp.Application.start/2` passes the
;     MODULE ATOM in the child list, so OTP asks THIS module for its own
;     generated `child_spec/1`; its `id` is the module atom and its `start` is
;     `{Module, :start_link, [[]]}`, which is what keeps the child's identity
;     the live module's. It is the `use GenServer` body exactly:
;     `Supervisor.child_spec(%{id: … , start: …}, [])`.
;
; Run: bin/bl test test/bl/host_loader_server_test.bl

(ns host.loader-server)

(defn start_link
  "The OTP `start_link/1`: start THE server under this module's own atom. The
   argument is ignored (the live module ignores it too); the child spec hands
   it `[]`."
  [_opts]
  (let [own (erlang/element 2 (erlang/fun_info (fn [] nil) :module))]
    (GenServer/start_link own :ok (list (tuple :name own)))))

(defn child_spec
  "The `child_spec/1` `use GenServer` generates, clause for clause: a map whose
   `id` is this module and whose `start` is `{this module, :start_link,
   [init_arg]}`, normalized by `Supervisor.child_spec/2` with the macro's empty
   option list. `init_arg` is the atom OTP was handed — for the application
   supervisor's child list that is `[]`, so `start_link/1` receives `[]`."
  [init_arg]
  (let [own (erlang/element 2 (erlang/fun_info (fn [] nil) :module))]
    (Supervisor/child_spec
      (maps/put :start (tuple own :start_link (list init_arg))
        (maps/put :id own (maps/new)))
      (list))))

(defn init
  "The OTP `init/1` callback: the server's state is an empty map. The live
   clause head is `init(:ok)`; any other argument is the BEAM's
   `FunctionClauseError`, raised here by hand."
  [arg]
  (if (erlang/== arg :ok)
    (tuple :ok (maps/new))
    (erlang/error
      (Elixir.FunctionClauseError/exception
        (list (tuple :module (erlang/element 2 (erlang/fun_info (fn [] nil) :module)))
              (tuple :function :init)
              (tuple :arity 1))))))

(defn in_server?
  "True when the CALLING process is the one running a `run/1` thunk — the live
   `Process.get(@flag) == true`. `Process.get/1` (not `:erlang.get/1`) is what
   turns an absent key into `nil` instead of the truthy atom `:undefined`."
  []
  (erlang/== true (Process/get :bl_in_loader_server)))

(defn- outcome [fun]
  (try (tuple :ok (erlang/apply fun (list)))
    (catch e
      (cond
        (erlang/== :throw __EXCEPTION_KIND__) (tuple :thrown e)
        (erlang/== :error __EXCEPTION_KIND__) (tuple :raised e __STACKTRACE__)
        :else (tuple :exited (erlang/element 2 e) __STACKTRACE__)))))

(defn- unwrap [reply]
  (case (erlang/element 1 reply)
    :ok (erlang/element 2 reply)
    :thrown (erlang/apply :erlang :throw (list (erlang/element 2 reply)))
    :raised (erlang/apply :erlang :raise
              (list :error (erlang/element 2 reply) (erlang/element 3 reply)))
    :exited (erlang/apply :erlang :raise
              (list :exit (erlang/element 2 reply) (erlang/element 3 reply)))))

(defn- bind_dependency [session fun]
  (BeamLisp.Env/convey (fn [arg]
    (let [previous (Process/get :bl_loader_dependency)]
      (Process/put :bl_loader_dependency session)
      (try (erlang/apply fun (list arg))
        (finally
          (if (erlang/== nil previous) (Process/delete :bl_loader_dependency)
            (Process/put :bl_loader_dependency previous))))))))

(defn- dependency_call [session fun]
  (let [owner (erlang/element 1 session)
        proxy (erlang/element 2 session)
        ticket (erlang/element 3 session)
        reply (erlang/make_ref)
        owner_monitor (erlang/monitor :process owner)
        proxy_monitor (erlang/monitor :process proxy)]
    (try
      ;; The owner's alias closes with the synchronous call. A retained or
      ;; racing stale callback cannot leave executable work in its mailbox.
      (erlang/send ticket (tuple :bl_loader_dependency ticket (erlang/self) reply fun))
      (receive
        [:bl_loader_dependency_reply ref result] :when (== ref reply)
          (unwrap result)
        [:DOWN ref :process _pid reason]
          :when (or (== ref owner_monitor) (== ref proxy_monitor))
          (erlang/error (tuple :loader_dependency_closed reason)))
      (finally
        (erlang/demonitor owner_monitor (list :flush))
        (erlang/demonitor proxy_monitor (list :flush))))))

(defn- await_worker [ticket monitor]
  ;; Selective receive leaves unrelated GenServer calls queued. Only this
  ;; synchronous handoff's descendants can ask the real loader to run code.
  (receive
    [:bl_loader_dependency ref caller reply fun] :when (== ref ticket)
      (let [result (outcome fun)]
        ;; A true exit remains fatal to the initializer's actual owner,
        ;; matching the ordinary inline run/1 branch.
        (if (erlang/== :exited (erlang/element 1 result)) (unwrap result)
          (do (erlang/send caller (tuple :bl_loader_dependency_reply reply result))
              (await_worker ticket monitor))))
    [:bl_loader_worker ref result] :when (== ref ticket)
      (do (erlang/demonitor monitor (list :flush)) (unwrap result))
    [:DOWN ref :process _pid reason] :when (== ref monitor)
      (erlang/error (tuple :loader_worker_closed reason))))

(defn worker_call
  "Run a synchronous worker call without a loader dependency cycle.
   `call` receives an Env-conveying callback wrapper; apply it to the callback
   submitted to the worker. Outside initialization this is a direct call. During
   initialization only that callback's causal nested loads are serviced, in
   the real loader process. Retaining the wrapper after the call is invalid."
  [call]
  (if (in_server?)
    (let [owner (erlang/self)
          context (BeamLisp.Env/capture)
          ticket (erlang/alias)
          spawned (erlang/spawn_monitor
            (fn []
              (let [proxy (erlang/self)
                    session (tuple owner proxy ticket)]
                (BeamLisp.Env/bind context)
                (Process/put :bl_loader_dependency session)
                ;; A monitor guardian also handles a normal owner exit. The
                ;; accepted Agent action drains independently of its waiter.
                (erlang/spawn
                  (fn []
                    (let [om (erlang/monitor :process owner)
                          pm (erlang/monitor :process proxy)]
                      (receive
                        [:DOWN ref :process _pid _reason] :when (== ref om)
                          (erlang/exit proxy :kill)
                        [:DOWN ref :process _pid _reason] :when (== ref pm) :ok))))
                (erlang/send owner (tuple :bl_loader_worker ticket
                  (outcome (fn [] (erlang/apply call
                    (list (fn [callback] (bind_dependency session callback)))))))))))]
      (try (await_worker ticket (erlang/element 2 spawned))
        (finally
          (erlang/unalias ticket)
          (erlang/demonitor (erlang/element 2 spawned) (list :flush)))))
    (let [session (Process/get :bl_loader_dependency)]
      (erlang/apply call (list (if session
        (fn [callback] (bind_dependency session callback))
        (fn [callback] (BeamLisp.Env/convey callback))))))))

(defn run
  "Run `fun` in the loader process, inline when the caller is already INSIDE it.

   The inline branch is the whole point: a library load that requires another
   library arrives here from the server's own process, and a `GenServer.call`
   to oneself would deadlock. `ensure_started/0` is inlined — it is private in
   the live module and, on this path, a top-level helper would become an export
   the live module never had. It races `Process.whereis/1` against
   `start_link/1` and treats OTP's `already_started` as success, because the
   server is also a supervised application child and may be up already.

   The reply is unwrapped exactly as the live `case` does: `{:ok, result}` is
   the result, `{:raised, e, stacktrace}` is re-raised with the caller's own
   `reraise/2` shape (with the ORIGINAL trace), `{:thrown, v}` is re-thrown as
   a genuine `:throw`. A non-zero-arity argument is the live guard's
   `FunctionClauseError`."
  [fun]
  (if (erlang/is_function fun 0)
    (if (Process/get :bl_in_loader_server)
      (erlang/apply fun (list))
      (let [own (erlang/element 2 (erlang/fun_info (fn [] nil) :module))]
        (if (erlang/== nil (Process/whereis own))
          (let [started (start_link (list))]
            (cond
              (and (erlang/is_tuple started)
                   (erlang/== :ok (erlang/element 1 started))) :ok

              (and (erlang/is_tuple started)
                   (erlang/== :error (erlang/element 1 started))
                   (erlang/is_tuple (erlang/element 2 started))
                   (erlang/== :already_started (erlang/element 1 (erlang/element 2 started)))) :ok

              :else
                (erlang/error
                  (Elixir.CaseClauseError/exception (list (tuple :term started))))))
          :ok)
        ;; This is the owning process boundary for every caller, including
        ;; direct AOT repair/build users. Search dirs have separate explicit
        ;; ownership; registered Env/project/osenv/diagnostics scopes convey.
        (let [admission (Process/get :bl_admission)
              modules (list (erlang/element 2 (erlang/fun_info fun :module)))
              action (BeamLisp.Env/convey
                (fn [_]
                  (if (or (erlang/== nil admission)
                          (and (erlang/is_map admission)
                               (erlang/is_pid (Map/get admission :owner))
                               (erlang/== (erlang/node (Map/get admission :owner)) (erlang/node))
                               (erlang/is_process_alive (Map/get admission :owner))
                               (erlang/is_integer (Map/get admission :deadline-ms))
                               (erlang/< (erlang/monotonic_time :millisecond) (Map/get admission :deadline-ms))))
                    ;; Accepted shared effects drain. Nested requires must not
                    ;; inherit a budget that can expire halfway through init.
                    (BeamLisp.Env/with_code_lease modules
                      (fn [] (Process/delete :bl_admission)
                             (erlang/apply fun (list))))
                    :cancelled)))
              reply (BeamLisp.Env/with_code_lease modules
                      (fn []
                        (let [session (Process/get :bl_loader_dependency)
                              run_action (fn [] (erlang/apply action (list nil)))]
                          (if session
                            ;; dependency_call unwraps for the worker; retain
                            ;; run/1's ordinary reply classification below.
                            (outcome (fn [] (dependency_call session run_action)))
                            (GenServer/call own (tuple :run run_action) :infinity)))))]
          (cond
            (and (erlang/is_tuple reply)
                 (erlang/== 2 (erlang/tuple_size reply))
                 (erlang/== :ok (erlang/element 1 reply)))
              (erlang/element 2 reply)

            (and (erlang/is_tuple reply)
                 (erlang/== 3 (erlang/tuple_size reply))
                 (erlang/== :raised (erlang/element 1 reply)))
              (erlang/apply :erlang :raise
                (list :error
                      (Elixir.Kernel.Utils/raise (erlang/element 2 reply))
                      (erlang/element 3 reply)))

            (and (erlang/is_tuple reply)
                 (erlang/== 2 (erlang/tuple_size reply))
                 (erlang/== :thrown (erlang/element 1 reply)))
              (erlang/apply :erlang :throw (list (erlang/element 2 reply)))

            (and (erlang/is_tuple reply)
                 (erlang/== 3 (erlang/tuple_size reply))
                 (erlang/== :exited (erlang/element 1 reply)))
              (erlang/apply :erlang :raise
                (list :exit (erlang/element 2 reply) (erlang/element 3 reply)))

            :else
              (erlang/error
                (Elixir.CaseClauseError/exception (list (tuple :term reply))))))))
    (erlang/error
      (Elixir.FunctionClauseError/exception
        (list (tuple :module (erlang/element 2 (erlang/fun_info (fn [] nil) :module)))
              (tuple :function :run)
              (tuple :arity 1))))))

(defn handle_call
  "The OTP `handle_call/3` callback for `{:run, fun}`: set the flag, run the
   thunk, classify what came back, clear the flag.

   The reply is the live module's three shapes — `{:ok, result}`,
   `{:raised, e, stacktrace}`, `{:thrown, v}` — and the caller unwraps them.
   Classification reads the RAW catch kind: `__EXCEPTION_KIND__` is the
   compiler-injected class of the catch (`:throw`, `:error` or `:exit`), so a
   raw `:erlang.throw/1` of an exception struct stays a throw, and one of an
   `{:exit, v}` pair stays a throw — the two corners the old shape-guess folded
   wrongly. `:error` answers `{:raised, e, __STACKTRACE__}`; `:exit` re-raises a
   TRUE exit through the dynamic `:erlang.raise/3` with the raw reason `v`
   recovered from the folded `{:exit, v}`.

   The clause head is `{:run, fun}` and nothing else: any other message is the
   BEAM's `FunctionClauseError`, raised here by hand. The `finally` deletes the
   flag on every exit path — including the exit re-raise — exactly as the live
   `after` does."
  [msg from state]
  (if (and (erlang/is_tuple msg)
           (erlang/== 2 (erlang/tuple_size msg))
           (erlang/== :run (erlang/element 1 msg)))
    (if (and (erlang/is_tuple from) (erlang/== 2 (erlang/tuple_size from))
             (erlang/is_pid (erlang/element 1 from))
             (erlang/== (erlang/node (erlang/element 1 from)) (erlang/node))
             (erlang/not (erlang/is_process_alive (erlang/element 1 from))))
      ;; Admission is the cancellation boundary. A dead queued caller buys no
      ;; source evaluation; an admitted initializer always drains normally.
      (tuple :reply (tuple :ok :cancelled) state)
      (do
      (Process/put :bl_in_loader_server true)
      (let [fun (erlang/element 2 msg)
            reply
            (try
              (tuple :ok (erlang/apply fun (list)))
              (catch e
                (cond
                  (erlang/== :throw __EXCEPTION_KIND__)
                    (tuple :thrown e)

                  (erlang/== :error __EXCEPTION_KIND__)
                    (tuple :raised e __STACKTRACE__)

                  (erlang/== :exit __EXCEPTION_KIND__)
                    (erlang/apply :erlang :raise
                      (list :exit (erlang/element 2 e) __STACKTRACE__))

                  :else
                    (tuple :thrown e)))
              (finally (Process/delete :bl_in_loader_server)))]
        (tuple :reply reply state))))
    (erlang/error
      (Elixir.FunctionClauseError/exception
        (list (tuple :module (erlang/element 2 (erlang/fun_info (fn [] nil) :module)))
              (tuple :function :handle_call)
              (tuple :arity 3))))))

(defn handle_cast
  "The `handle_cast/2` default `use GenServer` generates. `:erlang.phash2(1, 1)`
   is always `0`, so the reachable branch is the raise; the `{:stop, {:bad_cast,
   msg}, state}` branch exists so Dialyzer sees a non-local return. The message
   is the macro's, byte for byte, with the same registered-name-or-pid
   `inspect`."
  [msg state]
  (let [info (Process/info (erlang/self) :registered_name)
        proc (cond
               (and (erlang/is_tuple info)
                    (erlang/== 2 (erlang/tuple_size info))
                    (erlang/== () (erlang/element 2 info)))
                 (erlang/self)

               (and (erlang/is_tuple info) (erlang/== 2 (erlang/tuple_size info)))
                 (erlang/element 2 info)

               ; The live `case` has NO catch-all, so a `Process.info/2` answer
               ; that is neither shape is a CaseClauseError. A live `self()`
               ; always answers a 2-tuple, so this branch is the SHAPE of the
               ; live clause rather than a path either module can take.
               :else
                 (erlang/error
                   (Elixir.CaseClauseError/exception (list (tuple :term info)))))]
    (if (erlang/== 0 (erlang/phash2 1 1))
      (erlang/error
        (Elixir.RuntimeError/exception
          (erlang/iolist_to_binary
            (list "attempted to cast GenServer "
                  (Kernel/inspect proc)
                  " but no handle_cast/2 clause was provided"))))
      (tuple :stop (tuple :bad_cast msg) state))))

(defn handle_info
  "The `handle_info/2` default `use GenServer` generates: log the unhandled
   message through `:logger` with the macro's own label, report and metadata,
   then answer `{:noreply, state}`.

   The `report_cb` is `Elixir.Function/capture` rather than a closure on
   purpose: `&GenServer.format_report/1` is a MODULE-LOCAL fun, and capturing
   the remote function reproduces it exactly (`:erlang.fun_info` agrees on
   module, name, arity and type `:external`), where a hand-written closure would
   be a different, equally-callable fun that renders differently."
  [msg state]
  (let [info (Process/info (erlang/self) :registered_name)
        proc (cond
               (and (erlang/is_tuple info)
                    (erlang/== 2 (erlang/tuple_size info))
                    (erlang/== () (erlang/element 2 info)))
                 (erlang/self)

               (and (erlang/is_tuple info) (erlang/== 2 (erlang/tuple_size info)))
                 (erlang/element 2 info)

               ; as in `handle_cast/2`: the live `case` has no catch-all, and
               ; this is its shape, not a reachable path.
               :else
                 (erlang/error
                   (Elixir.CaseClauseError/exception (list (tuple :term info)))))
        report (maps/put :label (tuple :Elixir.GenServer :no_handle_info)
                 (maps/put :report
                   (maps/put :module (erlang/element 2 (erlang/fun_info (fn [] nil) :module))
                     (maps/put :message msg
                       (maps/put :name proc (maps/new))))
                   (maps/new)))
        metadata (maps/put :domain (list :otp :elixir)
                   (maps/put :error_logger (maps/put :tag :error_msg (maps/new))
                     (maps/put :report_cb (Elixir.Function/capture :Elixir.GenServer :format_report 1)
                       (maps/new))))]
    (erlang/apply :logger :error (list report metadata))
    (tuple :noreply state)))

(defn terminate
  "The `terminate/2` default `use GenServer` generates."
  [_reason _state]
  :ok)

(defn code_change
  "The `code_change/3` default `use GenServer` generates."
  [_old state _extra]
  (tuple :ok state))
